        "id": 1079868
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-05-02T17:30:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I am not sure about re-enabling after each wifi on-off.  I think the network will be re-enabled when the user tries to manually connect.  I think it might be better to wait for the user to re-enable the network since user action is most likely required to fix auth failures.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 4151,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 4151,
        "endChar": 73
      },
      "revId": "f8ee2445bc374945428ba9a6627506af0742081b",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6c27d495_74794276",
        "filename": "service/java/com/android/server/wifi/WifiStateMachine.java",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 4151,
      "author": {
        "id": 1116403
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-05-18T13:15:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\"re-enabling after each wifi on-off\" is actually requirement from operator. Also end users might expect everything is re-fleshed with wifi off-\u003eon. There would be auth failures caused by wifi network condition, in that case it\u0027s might be helpful to re-enable with wifi off-on. So I still think it\u0027s reasonable to keep it. What do you think?",
      "parentUuid": "808e236e_c0e82698",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 4151,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 4151,
        "endChar": 73
      },
      "revId": "f8ee2445bc374945428ba9a6627506af0742081b",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f904cc15_d5ea168a",
        "filename": "service/java/com/android/server/wifi/WifiStateMachine.java",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 4151,
      "author": {
        "id": 1079868
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-05-22T20:17:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think currently the only auth failures that might be depends on the wifi network condition is the auth timeout. We probably want to separate it out from the \"normal\" auth failures (e.g. wrong psk, EAP failure)\n\nOnce we remove the auth timeout from auth failures, an auth failure would indicate that there is a credential mis-match between the device and the AP, will likely not going to be fixed without updating credential on the device or the AP.",
      "parentUuid": "6c27d495_74794276",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 4151,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 4151,
        "endChar": 73
      },
      "revId": "f8ee2445bc374945428ba9a6627506af0742081b",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
